---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is a reverse proxy the same as a CDN?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "A CDN is a globally distributed reverse proxy with caching at hundreds of locations; a local Nginx proxy is the same concept on one machine."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does it break WebSockets?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Proxies need explicit upgrade handling for WebSockets; both Nginx and Cloudflare support it with configuration."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why terminate TLS at the proxy?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Centralized certificate management. Internal traffic between proxy and backend can then use a private PKI or plain HTTP on a trusted network."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Do I need one with a PaaS?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "No: Vercel/Netlify/Heroku already front your app with their own proxies and load balancers."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is X-Forwarded-For?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "The header a reverse proxy adds to carry the real client IP to your backend; trust it only from your own proxies, or attackers can spoof it."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Reverse Proxy Explained (Nginx, Cloudflare) | PromptDNS"
  description="Reverse proxies explained: how Nginx and Cloudflare sit in front of your servers, what they add, and how they differ from a load balancer."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ip-and-networking/" class="hover:text-accent">IP & Networking</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">Reverse Proxy</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">Reverse Proxy Explained</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A forward proxy hides clients; a reverse proxy hides servers. It sits in front of your infrastructure, receives all traffic, and forwards requests to your backend: adding TLS termination, caching, load balancing, and a security layer on the way.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">What it adds</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li class="leading-relaxed">TLS termination: one certificate at the proxy, plain HTTP to your backends internally.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Caching: static assets and hot responses served without touching the origin.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Load distribution: round-robin or health-checked forwarding across multiple backends.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Security: rate limiting, IP filtering, WAF integration, and hidden backend topology.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The examples you already know</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li class="leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-ink">Nginx:</strong> the classic self-hosted reverse proxy; proxy_pass directives route paths to upstreams.</li><li class="leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-ink">Cloudflare:</strong> a reverse proxy as a global service, with the orange cloud enabling it; DNS points at the edge, not your origin.</li><li class="leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-ink">Load balancers</strong> (AWS ALB, GCP LB) are reverse proxies specialized for distribution with health checks.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is a reverse proxy the same as a CDN?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A CDN is a globally distributed reverse proxy with caching at hundreds of locations; a local Nginx proxy is the same concept on one machine.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does it break WebSockets?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Proxies need explicit upgrade handling for WebSockets; both Nginx and Cloudflare support it with configuration.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why terminate TLS at the proxy?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Centralized certificate management. Internal traffic between proxy and backend can then use a private PKI or plain HTTP on a trusted network.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need one with a PaaS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: Vercel/Netlify/Heroku already front your app with their own proxies and load balancers.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is X-Forwarded-For?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The header a reverse proxy adds to carry the real client IP to your backend; trust it only from your own proxies, or attackers can spoof it.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/what-is-a-cdn/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">What is a CDN →</a></li><li><a href="/dns-load-balancing/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">DNS load balancing →</a></li><li><a href="/cname-flattening/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">CNAME flattening →</a></li></ul>

    <div class="mt-10 flex items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 px-5 py-3">
      <span class="grid h-9 w-9 place-items-center rounded-full bg-gradient-to-br from-accent to-violet font-display text-xs font-bold text-canvas">PD</span>
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">PromptDNS Team</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint">Last updated: August 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
